  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1238 AM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe weather dries out and warms up Tuesday through Thursday across \nsouthern Colorado.  There will still be sufficient moisture in \nsouthwest flow to support some isolated to scattered thunderstorms \non Tuesday, but these will be very hit or miss which should limit \nthe risk for flash flooding on burn scars.  Wednesday and Thursday \nlook even drier with temperatures on the far east plains pushing \ninto the upper 90s for highs by Thursday.  \n\nSoutherly flow around the southern U.S upper ridge sends another \nround of moisture northward into southern CO for Friday with \nprecipitable waters increasing to between 100-130% of normal. This \nshould bring a slight up tick in thunderstorm chances across the \narea by Friday, especially for the mountains. Precipitable waters \ncontinue to increase through next weekend bringing a better chance \nfor heavy rains and flash flooding, particularly along the \nContinental Divide.  Meanwhile, deep southwest flow aloft limits the \npersistence of low level moisture along the lower east facing slopes \nwith dew points dropping into the 40s in the afternoon.  This should \nreduce but not completely eliminate the flash flood risk on the \nAspen Acres burn scar.  Any storms that push off into the adjacent \nplains could produce gusty outflow winds up to 50 mph and lightning, \nalong with some brief moderate to heavy rainfall. -KT",
